  By projection formula, the resulting column map is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.15)")
   $ xor.big_(a_i gt.eq 0\
sum_i a_i = q) (⨂_(i = 1)^t H^0 \( W \, B + p a_i K_W \)) ⊗ cal(O)_(\( W' \)^t) (- sum_(i = 1)^t a_i K_(W' \, i)) arrow.r cal(E)^(times.square t) . $ <eq:diag-column-map>]
  Here $K_(W' \, i)$ is pulled back from slot $i$. The spanning assertion proves that this map has rank $R$ at one point, and hence at the generic point. The identity used is the line-bundle identity $F^(*) cal(O)_(W') \( K_(W') \) = cal(O)_W \( p K_W \)$; it is not the pullback map on differential forms.
  #emph[Rank loss along the diagonal.] On the diagonal $W' subset \( W' \)^t$, every source line bundle in (7.15) restricts to $cal(O)_(W') \( - q K_(W') \)$. Let
  $ Delta_F = underbrace(W times_(W') dots.h.c times_(W') W, t upright(" factors")) \, #h(2em) h : Delta_F arrow.r W' \, #h(2em) g : Delta_F arrow.r W $
  be the structure map and the first projection. Finite base change and projection formula identify
  $ h_(*) cal(O)_(Delta_F) (sum_i "pr"_i^(*) B + p q "pr"_1^(*) K_W) = \( cal(E)^(times.square t) \) \|_(W') ⊗ cal(O)_(W') \( q K_(W') \) . $
  After twisting, the columns restrict to global sections of this bundle, so their rank at every diagonal point is bounded by the dimension of that global section space.
  Filter by the ideal of the reduced diagonal in $Delta_F$ and push forward by $g$. The graded pieces are
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.16)")
   $ cal(G)_j = cal(O)_W \( t B + p q K_W \) ⊗ A_j \( \( Omega_W^1 \)^(xor \( t - 1 \)) \) \, quad 0 lt.eq j lt.eq n \( t - 1 \) \( p - 1 \) . $ <eq:diag-graded>]
  Indeed, in etale coordinates the $t - 1$ difference slots have $n \( t - 1 \)$ variables with $p$-th powers zero. Their degree-one transitions are those of the cotangent bundle. This is the many-factor form of the canonical Frobenius-diagonal filtration [39] Theorem 3.7, [24] Definition 3.1 and Corollary 3.5. If $e_j$ is the rank of the truncated factor, then $sum_j e_j = p^(n \( t - 1 \))$.
