  We have obtained the two rank bounds. It remains to apply the characteristic-zero curve test to a nonzero maximal minor, after specialization, and compare its order with this diagonal rank loss.
  #emph[The determinant contradiction.] Choose $R$ columns with nonzero determinant. If $a_i^(\( nu \))$ is the slot-$i$ weight in column $nu$, their determinant is a section of the external product of the line bundles
  $ cal(Q)_i = \( det cal(E) \)^(⊗ R \/ s) ⊗ cal(O)_(W') (sum_(nu = 1)^R a_i^(\( nu \)) K_(W')) . $
  Set $lambda_i = R^(- 1) sum_nu a_i^(\( nu \))$, so $0 lt.eq lambda_i lt.eq q$. Identifying numerical classes under the base-field twist,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.19)")
   $ frac(c_1 \( cal(Q)_i \), R) = B / p + (frac(p - 1, 2 p) + lambda_i) K_W . $ <eq:diag-determinant-class>]
  For a direct verification, the two-factor version of (7.16) filters $F^(*) cal(E)$ by $cal(O)_W \( B \) ⊗ A_j \( Omega_W^1 \)$. Among all $p^n = s$ truncated monomials, the total exponent of each variable is $s \( p - 1 \) \/ 2$. Hence
  $ c_1 \( F^(*) cal(E) \) = s B + frac(s \( p - 1 \), 2) K_W . $
  Frobenius pullback multiplies numerical divisor classes by $p$, which proves (7.19) and agrees with [39] Lemma 4.2.
  The normalized class in (7.19) pairs with the specialized test $beta$ to at most $D^(+) dot.op beta$, because $B \/ p$ is a nonnegative scalar multiple of $L$ no larger than $b L$, and the coefficient of $K_W$ is at most $q + 1$. For any nonzero slot section $s_i$ of $cal(Q)_i$, set $m_i = "ord"_(x') s_i$. Its divisor has effective strict transform of class $sigma^(*) c_1 \( cal(Q)_i \) - m_i J'$ on the twisted blow-up. Thus (7.13) gives
  $ m_i \( J dot.op beta \) lt.eq c_1 \( cal(Q)_i \) dot.op beta lt.eq R D^(+) dot.op beta < R d_0 \( J dot.op beta \) \, $
  and $m_i < R d_0$. The determinant is nonzero, so all its slot section spaces are nonzero. Lemma 26 gives the strict upper bound for its order at $\( x' \, dots.h \, x' \)$:
  $ R t d_0 = R b_0 \( C_1 + 1 \) < 3 R \/ 4 . $
  On the other hand, (7.18) lets us make at least $R - floor.l R \/ 4 floor.r$ rows of its matrix vanish at that point, by constant invertible row operations in local frames. Every determinant term then lies in the corresponding power of the maximal ideal, giving order at least $3 R \/ 4$. The contradiction proves (7.2). ◻
]

== 8 Compatible leaves and rational transport <sec:transport>

We now use the quadratic growth in Proposition 23 to finish the high-index contradiction. Low-cost curves on many diagonal products generate compatible leaves. Large covering degree forces these leaves to project generically finitely onto their images in every coordinate, so their dimensions stabilize. Their projection degrees cannot grow exponentially under a polynomial bound. A degree-one forgetting map then supplies rational evaluation of one coordinate from the others, and turns local flows into birational self-maps. This is the many-factor argument of [35] Section 8, in arbitrary fixed dimension.

#thm([Proposition 8.1.])[
  (The high-index contradiction).  #emph[There is no sequence of finite surjective covers $pi_nu : Y_nu arrow.r V_nu$ of normal projective complex $n$-folds, generically torsors for cyclic groups $G_nu$ of orders $r_nu arrow.r oo$, satisfying the following conditions. The groups $"Bir" \( V_nu \)$ are countable, and there are ample rational Cartier divisors $L_nu$ and constants $c \, C_2 > 0$, independent of $nu$, such that 
$ epsilon \( L_nu \) gt.eq c \, #h(2em) epsilon \( pi_nu^(*) L_nu \) gt.eq c r_nu^(1 \/ n) . $

 For $t gt.eq 2$, let $Z_(nu \, t) = Y_nu^t \/ G_(nu \, upright("diag"))$, let $theta_(nu \, t) : Z_(nu \, t) arrow.r V_nu^t$ be the finite quotient map, and set $P_(nu \, t) = theta_(nu \, t)^(*) L_nu^(plus.square t)$. The final condition is 
$ epsilon \( P_(nu \, t) \) lt.eq C_2 t^2 $

 for every fixed $t$, on a tail allowed to depend on $t$. All Seshadri constants are at very general points.]
] <prop:index-contradiction>

#proof[
  Fix an integer $T$, eventually large in terms of $n \, c \, C_2$. On a tail of the sequence, use the upper bounds for all $2 lt.eq t lt.eq T$ simultaneously. Suppress $nu$ until the covering degree must vary, and put $B = C_2 T^2 + 1$. Since $G$ is abelian, $theta_t$ is the finite quotient by $G^t \/ G_(upright("diag"))$: its further quotient has function field $bb(C) \( V^t \)$, and normality identifies it with $V^t$.
  #emph[Curves and projection-compatible leaves.] For each $t$, the strict bound $epsilon \( P_t \) < B$ supplies a marked covering family of integral curves with $P_t$-degree divided by marked multiplicity at most $B$. Choose a curve at geometric generic data and spread it with its mark. Equivalently, define the finite data over a countable field and realize the resulting generic families over $bb(C)$. Include every deck translate, every permutation of the slots, and every nonconstant image family under ordered coordinate projections to smaller sizes between $2$ and $T$. Closing under these operations still gives finite lists; no uniform bound on their sizes is needed.
  The cost cannot increase under projection. Indeed, for a nonconstant map $D arrow.r D'$ of integral curves of degree $e$, with marked point $z$ over $z'$, a general local hyperplane through $z'$ gives, on normalization,
  $ e "mult"_(z') D' gt.eq "mult"_z D . $
  The contribution from branches through $z$ cannot exceed the total over $z'$, and is at least $"mult"_z D$. Since $P_t$ minus the pullback of the lower polarization is nef, the degree inequality and this multiplicity inequality give the asserted cost comparison. The projected marks remain dominant; we take reduced integral geometric generic image curves.
  Apply Lemma 20 to these lists. Let $H_t$ be a geometric generic leaf and $h_t = dim H_t$. Then
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.1)")
   $ h_t > 0 \, #h(2em) P_t^(h_t) dot.op H_t lt.eq \( h_t B \)^(h_t) . $ <eq:transport-chain-degree-new>]
  Its finite-quotient functoriality shows that $W_t = theta_t \( H_t \)$ is the geometric generic leaf of a rational quotient $xi_t : V^t arrow.r.dashed S_t$ with geometrically integral generic fibre. These quotients and distributions are invariant under permutations of the slots.
  The image of an upper leaf under a coordinate projection lies in the corresponding lower leaf. To check the genericity in this assertion, retain the parameter of every upper family when forming its image family. Equality of the values of the lower quotient at a mark and a generic endpoint is an identity on that generic incidence. It remains valid with the additional history of any upper chain. Every upper step therefore preserves that quotient value; a stationary projected step does so immediately. This proves the inclusion for generic chains and then for their leaf closures. All comparisons below use a generic tuple and its projections, with their quotient parameter fields extended to a common algebraically closed field.
  #emph[Finiteness of the single-slot projections.] For fixed $T$ and sufficiently large $r$, each $H_t$ projects generically finitely onto its image in every $V$-slot. Suppose otherwise. For one slot write $I$ for the image and $j = dim I$, $a = h_t - j > 0$. The marked point of $I$ is ambient very general and generic on $I$ over the leaf’s quotient parameter field. Thus
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.2)")
   $ L^j dot.op I gt.eq c^j . $ <eq:transport-image-bound-new>]
  Here and below the degree bound follows by blowing up the marked point and taking the top intersection of the nef class given by the Seshadri bound on the strict transform of the subvariety. The generic point is smooth on the subvariety and has multiplicity one. For $j = 0$ the assertion reads $1 gt.eq 1$.
  Over a geometric generic point of this slot, the fibre of $Z_t arrow.r V$ identifies with $Y^(t - 1)$: choose one lift in the torsor to fix the first coordinate. The polarization restricts to the external sum of the remaining copies of $pi^(*) L$. Projecting a curve through the marked tuple to a nonconstant slot, using the preceding degree–multiplicity comparison, shows that this restricted polarization has Seshadri constant at least $c r^(1 \/ n)$ there. For an $a$-dimensional component $J$ of the geometric generic fibre of $H_t arrow.r I$ through the mark, we obtain $P_t^a dot.op J gt.eq \( c r^(1 \/ n) \)^a$. The projection formula and nefness of $P_t - "pr"^(*) L$ now give
  $ P_t^(h_t) dot.op H_t gt.eq \( "pr"^(*) L \)^j P_t^a dot.op H_t gt.eq c^j \( c r^(1 \/ n) \)^a . $
  This contradicts (8.1) as $r arrow.r oo$, since $h_t lt.eq n T$ and $T$ is fixed. Only finitely many slots and sizes are involved, so the conclusion holds simultaneously on a tail. In particular,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.3)")
   $ 1 lt.eq h_t lt.eq n \, $ <eq:transport-dimension-new>]
  and the same single-slot finiteness holds for $W_t$.
  #emph[A degree-one forgetting map.] Let $d_t$ be the degree of $W_t$ over its first-slot image. The degree of $H_t$ over that image contains $d_t$ as a factor. Combining (8.1) with (8.2) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.4)")
   $ d_t lt.eq K T^(2 n) \, #h(2em) K = max_(1 lt.eq h lt.eq n) (frac(h \( C_2 + 1 \), c))^h . $ <eq:transport-degree-new>]
  The constant $K$ is independent of $T$. Projection compatibility and finite first-slot projection imply $h_t lt.eq h_(t - 1)$ for $t gt.eq 3$. Whenever equality holds, the upper leaf maps onto the integral lower leaf; their first-slot images coincide and
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.5)")
   $ d_t \/ d_(t - 1) = deg \( W_t arrow.r W_(t - 1) \) in bb(N) . $ <eq:transport-ratio-new>]
  There is a constant stretch in the $T - 1$ dimensions of length at least $\( T - 1 \) \/ n$. Choose $T$ so large that
  $ \( T - 1 \) \/ n - 1 > log_2 \( K T^(2 n) \) . $
  If all the adjacent degrees on that stretch were at least two, (8.5) would contradict (8.4). Hence for some $k gt.eq 3$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.6)")
   $ h_k = h_(k - 1) = h > 0 \, #h(2em) W_k arrow.r W_(k - 1) med upright("is birational") . $ <eq:transport-degree-one-new>]
  Permutation invariance gives the same conclusion for every single-slot omission.
  We now have a positive-dimensional leaf which determines each missing coordinate rationally. The rest of the proof turns this rational recovery into too many birational transformations of $V$.
  #emph[Transport of tangent directions.] The tangent distribution $cal(D)_k = ker \( d xi_k \)$ has rank $h$ at a generic tuple. It projects injectively to each single-slot tangent space, and isomorphically to the lower distribution under every omission. These assertions follow from separability and the generically finite maps of geometric generic leaves. Let $A_i subset T_(V \, x_i)$ be its slot-$i$ image. It depends rationally only on $x_i$. Indeed, in a rational Grassmann chart its coordinates belong to the function field of every $\( k - 1 \)$-tuple containing slot $i$, so they belong to the intersection of these fields, namely $bb(C) \( V \)$ in slot $i$. Likewise, the transport from $A_i$ to $A_j$, obtained by lifting to $cal(D)_k$ and projecting to slot $j$, belongs to the field of the pair $\( x_i \, x_j \)$.
  The field intersection just used is elementary for independent product variables. Replace an omitted variable by a fresh independent generic variable. A rational function pulled from the remaining slots is unchanged by this replacement. Repeating and then specializing the fresh variables to general constants shows that it is pulled from the common slots. Applied to the Grassmann and matrix chart coordinates, this proves both descent statements, including when $k = 3$.
  Permutation symmetry therefore gives one rational plane distribution $A \( x \)$ and rational isomorphisms $R \( x \, y \) : A \( x \) arrow.r A \( y \)$. Lifting a vector to the same leaf tangent space and using three slots gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.7)")
   $ R \( y \, z \) R \( x \, y \) = R \( x \, z \) . $ <eq:transport-cocycle-new>]
  Choose a general reference point $a in V \( bb(C) \)$ and transport a basis of $A \( a \)$ by $R \( a \, x \)$. This produces rational vector fields $v_1 \, dots.h \, v_h$, independent at a generic point, whose simultaneous copies $\( v_i \, dots.h \, v_i \)$ span $cal(D)_k$ by (8.7). No commutativity is asserted or needed.
  #emph[Rational recovery and local flows.] Write $xi = xi_k$ and let $D$ be the image closure of
  $ Lambda = \( "pr"_(< k) \, xi \) : V^k arrow.r.dashed D . $
  This map is birational. To see that no algebraic degree remains, write $F_0 subset E subset F$ for the function fields of $S_k \, D \, V^k$. The field $E$ is generated over $F_0$ by the first $k - 1$ slots, and (8.6) shows that $F \/ E$ is finite. Geometric integrality of the generic leaf means that $F \/ F_0$ is regular. After extension to an algebraic closure of $F_0$, the corresponding generic fields therefore stay fields, and the degree of $F \/ E$ remains unchanged. The geometric forgetting map has degree one, so $F = E$. Let $"ev"$ be the last coordinate of the rational inverse of $Lambda$.
  Choose a general complex tuple $\( u \, y_0 \)$, with $u in V^(k - 1)$, in smooth opens where the vector fields, quotient, and rational evaluation are regular. For small $z = \( z_1 \, dots.h \, z_h \) in bb(C)^h$, let $E_z$ be the ordered composition of the time-$z_i$ local holomorphic flows of $v_i$. Their coefficients are holomorphic on the chosen opens, so the local existence and uniqueness theorem for differential equations gives the flows uniformly on smaller neighbourhoods. The reversed composition of the negative-time flows is their local inverse $E_z^(-)$.
  The simultaneous flows preserve $xi$. Consequently, for $y$ near $y_0$ and sufficiently small $z$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.8)")
   $ E_z \( y \) = "ev" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] E_z \( u \) \, xi \( u \, y \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <eq:transport-flow-new>]
  For each fixed $z$ the right side is rational in $y$. Equality on an analytic open proves that its input lies in $D$ and meets the domain of evaluation; that analytic open is Zariski dense. Thus it defines a rational self-map of $V$, regular at $y_0$ after shrinking the times. The identical argument for $E_z^(-)$ gives a rational inverse, because the two compositions agree with the identity on smaller analytic opens. Each $E_z$ is therefore birational. Since $h > 0$ and the $v_i \( y_0 \)$ are independent, the map $z mapsto E_z \( y_0 \)$ has nonzero derivative and uncountable image. This produces uncountably many distinct members of $"Bir" \( V \)$, a contradiction. ◻
]

Apply this proposition to the sequence in Proposition 16. Choose $L$ rationally scaled so that $1 lt.eq L^n lt.eq 2$. Proposition 19 gives uniform $gamma \( L \) lt.eq C_1$, $epsilon \( L \) gt.eq c$, and $epsilon \( pi^(*) L \) gt.eq c r^(1 \/ n)$, since $\( pi^(*) L \)^n = r L^n$. Proposition 23 supplies the remaining bound with $C_2 = \( n + 1 \) \/ b_0$, while countability of $"Bir" \( V \)$ is part of Proposition 16. The high-index sequence is impossible. Together with the other cases of that reduction, this proves the zero-boundary klt canonical-index bound in dimension $n$.

== 9 Completion of the induction <sec:completion>

We now assemble the two uniform statements. Their proofs have deliberately been separated: the scalar estimates use effective Iitaka fibrations only in smaller dimensions, whereas the denominator bound uses normal lc indices only in smaller dimensions.

#proof[
  Assume $I_j \, L_j$ for $j < n$. For $n = 1$, a normal projective curve with torsion canonical class is a smooth curve of genus one, so $K_1$ holds with index one. For $n gt.eq 2$, Proposition 16 reduces failure of $K_n$ to the terminal high-index sequence considered in the diagonal-product argument. Proposition 19 supplies the required uniform section-order and Seshadri estimates. Proposition 27 excludes that sequence. Therefore $K_n$ holds in every dimension. Proposition 6, together with $L_j$ for $j < n$, now proves $L_n$ for every finite rational coefficient set. Global ACC gives its rational DCC version as explained in Section 2. ◻
]

Before passing to the Iitaka map, we spell out why no unbounded Cartier index interferes with the comparison of section spaces.

#thm([Lemma 9.1.])[
  (All-degree canonical comparison).  #emph[Let $X$ be a smooth projective complex variety with pseudo-effective $K_X$. There is a projective terminal good minimal model $V$ of $X$. For every integer $m gt.eq 0$, its natural birational identification of function fields identifies 
$ H^0 \( X \, cal(O)_X \( m K_X \) \) = H^0 \( V \, cal(O)_V \( m K_V \) \) . $

 The right-hand side is a divisorial section space even when $m K_V$ is not Cartier.]
] <lem:all-degree>

#proof[
  Theorem 3 and the klt MMP give a good minimal model; starting from a smooth variety, the discrepancy comparisons keep the model terminal. Let $W$ be a common smooth resolution. Since $V$ is canonical, compatible canonical divisors satisfy
  $ K_W = q^(*) K_V + A \, #h(2em) A gt.eq 0 $
  for $q : W arrow.r V$, with $A$ exceptional. A rational $m$-canonical form regular as a divisorial section on $V$ has an effective zero divisor there. This divisor is rational Cartier: it differs from $m K_V$ by a principal divisor. Its pullback is effective, and adding $m A$ shows that the form is regular on $W$. This assertion is about actual integral orders of the form; it does not require $m K_V$ to be Cartier. Conversely, a form regular on $W$ is regular at the generic point of every prime divisor on $V$, and hence is a divisorial section there by normality. Thus all degrees agree on $V$ and $W$. The same argument for the birational morphism $W arrow.r X$ identifies the spaces on $W$ and $X$. Section ratios are preserved by these identifications. ◻
]

#proof[
  We complete the simultaneous induction step. Let $X$ be a smooth projective $n$-fold with $kappa \( X \) gt.eq 0$, and take $V$ as in Lemma 28. The semiample canonical divisor defines a contraction $f : V arrow.r Z$ and a rational ample divisor $D$ on $Z$ with $K_V tilde.op_(bb(Q)) f^(*) D$. This contraction represents the Iitaka fibration.
  If $Z$ is a point, $K_V tilde.op_(bb(Q)) 0$, and $L_n$, just proved, gives a common nonempty pluricanonical degree. Its image is a point. If $dim Z > 0$, Proposition 10 applies. Its base adjoint is rationally linearly equivalent to $D$, so it is big. Corollary 13 supplies a degree depending only on $n$ whose section ratios give all of $bb(C) \( Z \)$. The all-degree comparison transfers this conclusion to $X$.
  There are only finitely many possible base dimensions. Choose a common multiple of the degrees supplied for these cases and for the point case. Passing to this common multiple preserves the full section field: if $s_0 eq.not 0$ lies in the earlier degree and the new degree is $q$ times that degree, the ratio $s \/ s_0$ is also the ratio $\( s s_0^(q - 1) \) \/ s_0^q$ in the new complete system. All ratios in the new system still lie in the Iitaka field by its definition. This proves $I_n$ and closes the simultaneous induction starting from dimension zero. ◻
]

=== 9.1 Algebraically closed fields of characteristic zero <algebraically-closed-fields-of-characteristic-zero>

Let $k$ be any algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. A given variety or pair and its finite defining data descend to a finitely generated subfield $k_0 subset k$. Let $overline(k_0)$ be an algebraic closure inside $k$ and embed it into $bb(C)$. Smoothness, integrality, singularity conditions, and the relevant section spaces are unchanged by these algebraically closed extensions. For Theorem 2, include a rational principalization of a sufficiently divisible multiple among the descended data. The uniform trivialization over $bb(C)$ descends by the line-bundle argument in Section 2 and extends to $k$.

For Theorem 1, include one nonzero pluricanonical section among the defining data to preserve nonnegative Kodaira dimension. The same integer $m \( n \)$ gives a nonempty system after every extension. To check its field, choose a sufficiently large divisible degree $r$ which also is divisible by $m \( n \)$ and defines the Iitaka fibration. The products used in the preceding proof give a dominant rational map from the degree-$r$ image to the degree-$m \( n \)$ image. It is birational if and only if it is birational after an algebraically closed extension: equivalently, the corresponding function-field extension has degree one, a property detected by faithful flatness. It is birational after comparison with $bb(C)$, hence over $k$. The same dimension-dependent integers therefore work over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero.

